; datom/store.bl — the STORAGE PORT: the only thing that knows a substrate.
;
; ── Why this file is the most important boundary in the system ─────────
;
; Everything above it (datoms, indexes, transactions, datalog) is pure
; logic over an ordered key/value space. Everything below it is somebody
; else's storage engine. This protocol is the seam, and it is deliberately
; TINY — six operations — because every method here is a method that a
; future substrate must implement, and a fat port is a port nothing else
; can ever fill.
;
; The plan (PLAN-033) targets Hobbes as the eventual substrate: a
; FoundationDB-architecture distributed store, in Elixir, with strictly
; serializable transactions over an ordered keyspace. Hobbes is pre-alpha
; and has NO PUBLIC API yet, so its shape WILL churn. That risk is
; contained precisely here: `store-ets` is the substrate the database is
; developed and tested against, `store-hobbes` arrives later behind the
; same six methods, and no layer above ever learns which one it has.
;
; ── Why ORDERED matters (the one non-obvious requirement) ─────────────
;
; A hash store answers "what is at key K?". An EAV index needs "every key
; between K1 and K2, in order" — because a covering index IS an
; order-preserving key encoding plus a range scan over it. Pattern
; `[?e :person/age ?a]` becomes a scan of the AEVT range prefixed by
; :person/age. Without ordered range scans there is no index, only a full
; table scan wearing a costume.
;
; ∴ `-range` is the load-bearing method. `-get` is a convenience that
; could be expressed as a one-element range; it exists because point
; lookups are frequent enough to deserve a direct path.

(ns datom.store)

(defprotocol Store
  "An ordered, transactional key/value space.

   Keys are byte-comparable encoded terms (see datom/codec.bl): the
   ordering of the ENCODED key is what indexes rely on, so a substrate
   must compare keys as opaque sortable values and never reinterpret
   them.

   Implementations must satisfy the conformance suite in
   test/bl/datom/store_test.bl — that suite is the actual contract, and
   it runs unchanged against every substrate."

  (-get [store k]
    "The value at `k`, or nil when absent.

     nil is unambiguous here because the layers above never STORE nil: a
     retracted datom is represented by absence, and an index entry's
     value is a non-nil marker. Substrates therefore need not distinguish
     'absent' from 'present but nil'.")

  (-range [store start stop]
    "All `[k v]` entries with `start <= k <= stop`, in ascending key
     order. `nil` on either bound means unbounded on that side.

     Bounds are INCLUSIVE — the same convention as `subseq` in core, so
     the two compose without an off-by-one translation layer at the seam.

     The result must be ordered. A substrate that cannot guarantee order
     cannot back an index and must not implement this protocol.")

  (-put [store k v]
    "Store `v` at `k`, replacing any existing value. Returns the store.

     Stores are VALUES to the layers above where the substrate allows it
     (the ETS impl returns a handle; a persistent impl may return a new
     store). Callers must always use the RETURN value, never assume
     mutation in place — that discipline is what lets a persistent
     substrate drop in without rewriting callers.")

  (-delete [store k]
    "Remove `k` (idempotent). Returns the store.")

  (-update [store k f]
    "ATOMICALLY replace the value at `k` with `(f current)`, where
     `current` is nil when absent. Returns `[store new-value]`.

     The sixth method, and the one that is NOT expressible in terms of
     the others. `-get` then `-put` is a read-modify-write RACE: two
     writers both read `t=41`, both write `t=42`, and the database has
     two transactions claiming one basis. Datomic avoids this with a
     single transactor; on the BEAM the natural equivalent is a
     single-writer process per database, but that is a policy the LAYER
     ABOVE chooses — the port still has to offer an atomic primitive so
     a substrate that HAS one (Hobbes: strict serializability) can supply
     it rather than being forced through a lock it does not need.

     Wave 2 needs this for the `:db.part/tx` counter; wave 4 needs it for
     `:db/unique` upsert, where 'does this value already exist?' and
     'claim it' must be one indivisible step.

     `f` must be PURE and may be retried by a substrate that implements
     this as an optimistic CAS loop.")

  (-commit [store ops]
    "Apply a batch of operations ATOMICALLY. Returns the store.

     `ops` is a sequence of `[:put k v]` and `[:delete k]` vectors.
     Either every operation is visible or none is — a transaction that
     writes four index entries for one datom must never be observable
     with two of them written.

     This is the method that makes the EAV layer's transactions real, and
     it is why the port targets a TRANSACTIONAL substrate rather than a
     plain KV store. An implementation that cannot offer atomicity (a
     bare file, say) must say so loudly rather than silently degrading —
     a torn index is a corrupt database, not a slow one."))

;; ── the datom-scan capability (separate from the 6-method port) ──────
;;
;; The Store port above is deliberately tiny and substrate-agnostic: it
;; knows keys and values, never datoms. But a datom index READ has one
;; extra degree of freedom a substrate can exploit — the values in a
;; datom range are all datoms, and a backend that serializes them (a serializing backend like fjall)
;; can decode a whole range in one native crossing instead of one BEAM
;; `binary_to_term` per row. That is the ~40% read tax Fix A removes.
;;
;; This is a SEPARATE, optional protocol rather than a 7th Store method,
;; so the load-bearing port stays fillable by a bare KV substrate. A
;; backend that does not implement it falls back — not to a slower engine,
;; but to the same range read the index always did (`scan-via-range`),
;; which for an in-memory store returns live datoms at no decode cost.

(defprotocol Scan
  "A substrate's fast path for reading a whole datom range as datoms."
  (-scan-datoms [store lo hi]
    "Every datom whose key is in `[lo, hi]` (inclusive), in key order.
     Returns datoms `[e a v tx op]`, NOT `[k v]` pairs — the caller wants
     the facts, and a substrate that can produce them without a per-row
     BEAM decode should. No filtering: retraction and time-travel
     filtering are the db layer's basis-aware job."))

(defn scan-via-range
  "The default `-scan-datoms`: read the range and take the values. For an
   in-memory substrate the values ARE live datoms, so this is already the
   fast path; only a serializing substrate (a serializing backend like fjall) benefits from an
   override."
  [store lo hi]
  (mapv (fn [entry] (get entry 1)) (-range store lo hi)))

(defn scan-datoms
  "Read a datom range as datoms, using the substrate's fast path when it
   has one. This is the single entry the index layer calls: it dispatches
   on CAPABILITY (does this store implement `Scan`?), not on the store's
   identity, so adding a substrate never edits this function and no query
   path carries an `if a durable backend …` branch."
  [store lo hi]
  (if (satisfies? Scan store)
    (-scan-datoms store lo hi)
    (scan-via-range store lo hi)))

;; ── the multi-prefix capability ───────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; A JOIN over an already-bound variable wants neither shape above. It has a
;; SET of prefixes — one per distinct join value — and wants every datom under
;; any of them. Reading the whole column and filtering is O(column); reading
;; one range per prefix is O(answer) in rows but N store requests. `MultiScan`
;; is the third shape: N prefixes in, their datoms out, ONE request.
;;
;; The engine's `pattern-scan-once` chooses it when the join variable is
;; bound in every binding row (the semi-join case). Measured on a 64 708-row
;; column joined against 1 760 bound entities: the column path read 129 416
;; datoms to keep 3 520; this reads 3 520.
;;
;; Like `Scan`, optional — but UNLIKE `Scan`, the engine does not fall back
;; to N single ranges when the capability is absent: that would reintroduce
;; the N+1 request shape `cost_test` forbids. A substrate without `MultiScan`
;; gets the one-column-scan path instead (`multi-scan?` is the engine's
;; question). `scan-via-prefixes` exists for callers that KNOW they want the
;; rows regardless of request count (tests, in-memory stores).

(defprotocol MultiScan
  "A substrate's one-request read of several datom prefixes."
  (-scan-prefixes [store bounds]
    "Every datom whose key lies in any of `bounds`, a vector of `[lo hi]`
     inclusive pairs, in the order the pairs were given (and key order within
     each). Datoms `[e a v tx op]`, unfiltered, exactly as `-scan-datoms`."))

(defn scan-via-prefixes
  "The default `-scan-prefixes`: one `scan-datoms` per bound, concatenated.
   Same rows as the native path; N requests instead of one."
  [store bounds]
  (reduce (fn [acc b] (reduce conj acc (scan-datoms store (get b 0) (get b 1))))
          []
          bounds))

(defn multi-scan?
  "Whether `store` reads several prefixes in ONE request. The engine
   chooses the semi-join read only when this holds."
  [store]
  (satisfies? MultiScan store))

(defn scan-prefixes
  "Read several datom prefixes, using the substrate's multi-range fast path
   when it has one. Dispatches on CAPABILITY, like `scan-datoms`."
  [store bounds]
  (if (satisfies? MultiScan store)
    (-scan-prefixes store bounds)
    (scan-via-prefixes store bounds)))

;; ── the bounded-page capability ──────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; `Scan` reads a whole datom range at once. An incremental backfill wants the
;; opposite shape: walk one attribute's entities in BOUNDED pages, never
;; holding the column. `PageScan` is that optional capability, and it is what
;; makes a durable attribute walk cost the PAGE rather than the corpus.
;;
;; The contract mirrors `Scan`'s inclusive bounds and the native chunked read
;; a serializing backend already exposes: one call returns AT MOST `limit`
;; datoms from `[start, hi]`, resuming strictly after `after` when `start` is
;; nil — exactly one of the two lower bounds is used, as `fjall-resolve-chunk`
;; specifies. `:last` is the key of the final datom read (pass it back as
;; `after` to continue) and is nil exactly when the page is empty; `:end?` is
;; true when the range held fewer than `limit` datoms, so the caller stops
;; there rather than by counting the rows it asked for.
;;
;; A substrate that cannot page falls back to `scan-via-range-page`, which is
;; CORRECT but materializes the whole range first — the documented fallback,
;; never what a large durable column should take.

(defprotocol PageScan
  "A substrate's bounded page read over a datom range."
  (-scan-datoms-page [store start after hi limit]
    "At most `limit` datoms in key order from `[start, hi]` (inclusive), or
     strictly after `after` when `start` is nil. Returns
     `{:datoms [...] :last key-or-nil :end? bool}`. `:end?` is true when the
     range held fewer than `limit` datoms (nothing more follows); `:last` is
     nil exactly when the page was empty."))

(defn scan-via-range-page
  "The default `-scan-datoms-page`: read the range, keep at most `limit`
   entries after `start`/`after`.

   CORRECT for every substrate, but it materializes the WHOLE range before it
   returns a page — the documented perf fallback for an in-memory store with
   no bounded primitive. A large durable column must not take this path; that
   is what the `PageScan` override is for."
  [store start after hi limit]
  (let [entries (-range store (or start after) hi)
        ; `-range` bounds are inclusive, so resuming from `after` re-reads the
        ; last key; drop it. A key since deleted is simply absent, and the
        ; filter drops nothing.
        entries (if (and (nil? start) (some? after))
                  (filterv (fn [e] (not= after (get e 0))) entries)
                  entries)
        page (vec (take limit entries))]
    {:datoms (mapv (fn [e] (get e 1)) page)
     :last (if (empty? page) nil (get (peek page) 0))
     :end? (< (count page) limit)}))

(defn page-datoms
  "Read a bounded page of a datom range, using the substrate's paging fast
   path when it has one. The single entry the db layer calls: it dispatches on
   CAPABILITY (does this store implement `PageScan`?), so no caller names a
   substrate and an in-memory store without one still answers correctly."
  [store start after hi limit]
  (if (satisfies? PageScan store)
    (-scan-datoms-page store start after hi limit)
    (scan-via-range-page store start after hi limit)))

;; ── FileBacked: the on-disk naming convention of a persistent store ──
;;
;; A durable substrate lives at a filesystem PATH, and each engine has its own
;; convention: fjall is a DIRECTORY (a keyspace of segment files with a `.fjall` suffix).
;; That convention is knowledge the STORE owns, not the caller. Before this, a test helper
;; hardcoded a single backend's suffix when minting a fresh path — a leak that silently
;; mislabels a fjall directory and, worse, bakes one backend's shape into generic code.
;;
;; Like `Scan`, this is a SEPARATE optional protocol, not a Store method: an
;; in-memory store has no path and simply does not implement it. `path-suffix`
;; is a pure descriptor (a string), so a caller composes a unique path without
;; knowing which engine it will hand to.

(defprotocol FileBacked
  "A persistent store's on-disk naming convention."
  (-path-suffix [store]
    "The suffix a fresh path for THIS engine should carry, e.g. \".fjall\" for a
     directory-keyspace store. A pure descriptor: it names the convention, it does not
     touch the filesystem."))

(defn file-backed?
  "Whether `store` (or a store VALUE from this engine) declares a file-backed
   naming convention."
  [store]
  (satisfies? FileBacked store))

(defn fresh-path
  "A filesystem path unique across VMs AND within one run, carrying `suffix`
   (the engine's convention from `-path-suffix`). The uniqueness is the whole
   point: two `bl test` runs restart `unique_integer` from the same
   values, so a name built from that alone lets run N reopen run N-1's durable
   files — a persistent store that should hold one key then holds two, a flake
   that reads as 'the database is unreliable'. `os/getpid` distinguishes the VM,
   the monotonic clock distinguishes a recycled pid, `unique_integer`
   distinguishes calls within one run: all three, each covering a case the
   others do not. This lives here, ONCE, instead of being copied (with a
   hardcoded suffix) into every test that needs a durable store."
  [suffix]
  (str "/tmp/beam_lisp_store_"
       (erlang/list_to_binary (os/getpid))
       "_"
       (erlang/monotonic_time :nanosecond)
       "_"
       (erlang/unique_integer (to-list [:positive]))
       suffix))

(defn put-all
  "Apply `[k v]` entries to `store` atomically via `-commit`.

   A convenience over the protocol, not a sixth method: it lowers to the
   same `-commit` every substrate already implements."
  [store entries]
  (-commit store (mapv (fn [e] [:put (get e 0) (get e 1)]) entries)))

(defn range-keys
  "Just the keys of `(-range store start stop)`, in order."
  [store start stop]
  (mapv (fn [e] (get e 0)) (-range store start stop)))

(defn next-id
  "Atomically increment the counter at `k` and return the new value.

   The canonical `-update` use: entity- and transaction-id allocation.
   Starts at 1 when the counter does not yet exist."
  [store k]
  (get (-update store k (fn [current] (if (nil? current) 1 (inc current)))) 1))

(defn range-count
  "How many entries lie between the bounds.

   Deliberately NOT a protocol method: a substrate with a cheap counting
   primitive can be given one later, but requiring it up front would make
   the port harder to fill for no present gain."
  [store start stop]
  (count (-range store start stop)))

;; ── Closeable: a substrate that owns something needing teardown ──────
;;
;; A port has no close: reads and writes are the contract, lifetime is not.
;; But SOME substrates own a resource the VM will not reclaim by itself — an
;; ETS table (lives until its owner dies), a temp directory (lives forever),
;; a journal that wants one last sync. Those declare it here, and
;; `datom.conn/close!` tears them down after releasing the registry entry.
;;
;; Optional, like `Scan` and `FileBacked`: a store with nothing to free simply
;; does not implement it. Shared by BOTH ports — a blob store (datom.blob) is
;; a substrate resource in exactly the same sense, so it extends this rather
;; than a second protocol of the same shape.

(defprotocol Closeable
  "A substrate resource with explicit teardown."
  (-close [resource]
    "Free what this resource owns. IDEMPOTENT: a second close is a no-op, so
     a caller never has to know whether it already happened. Durable data is
     never destroyed here — a persistent store syncs and lets go; only
     EPHEMERAL resources (memory tables, temp dirs) are deleted."))

(defn close
  "Close `resource` if it declares teardown; otherwise nothing."
  [resource]
  (if (satisfies? Closeable resource) (-close resource) nil))

;; `Closeable` says how a resource is torn down; `Alive?` asks whether one is
;; still there to be used. Same lifetime, opposite end, and only some substrates
;; can answer the second: an ETS table is OWNED by the process that opened it
;; (see `store-ets/open`), so it dies with that process, while a sorted map in an
;; atom has no owner to outlive. Optional like `Closeable` and `Scan` — and the
;; default is `true`, which is the honest answer for a handle that cannot die on
;; its own. A caller that MEMOIZES a resource across process boundaries is the
;; audience: a memo is only worth keeping while this says so.

(defprotocol Alive?
  "A substrate resource whose handle can outlive what it names."
  (-alive? [resource]
    "Is `resource` still backed by what it names? Must answer for a dead
     handle rather than raising — that case is the reason it exists."))

(defn alive?
  "Is `resource` still usable? A resource that cannot die by itself, is."
  [resource]
  (if (satisfies? Alive? resource) (-alive? resource) true))

